.TH CCOL_R_MEMPOOL_FREE_ENTRY 3 "2026-09-17" "c_collections" "C Collections Library - Macros"
.SH NAME
ccol_r_mempool_free_entry \- free an entry back to the ranged pool
.SH LIBRARY
C Collections (libccollections, \-lccollections)
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
#include <ccollections/cmempool.h>

ccol_r_mempool_free_entry(rmp, entry);
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
.nf
Returns an allocated entry to the sub-pool that owns it. It then sets the
pointer of the caller to NULL.

The caller names the ranged pool. The macro does not derive it from the
entry. The macro resolves which sub-pool owns the entry from the address of
that entry. The caller therefore never has to track that. Free an entry to
the ranged pool that it came from. To pass a different pool is a caller
error, and it is fatal.
.fi
.SH PARAMETERS
.nf
rmp    Ranged pool the entry was allocated from
entry  Entry to free (will be set to NULL after freeing)
.fi
.SH NOTES
.nf
The search for the owning sub-pool visits each tier at most once; the
  free itself is O(1)
It works for an entry from any sub-pool in the ranged pool
It also works for an entry that the dynamic fallback allocated
It is safe to call with a NULL entry. A NULL pool is a caller error, and it
  is fatal
It detects corruption with assertions

The macro resolves which sub-pool owns the entry from the address of that
entry, and it reads nothing through an address that no sub-pool owns. An
entry of the dynamic fallback is found in the record of fallback entries
that its sub-pool keeps. An entry of another pool, and a fallback entry
that is already free, end in the assert. See ccol_mempool_free_entry(3)
and ccol_r_mempool_create(3).

The macro evaluates each argument exactly once. entry must be a
modifiable lvalue, such as a variable or an element of an array, so an
expression with a side effect, such as entries[i++], frees and clears
one element.
.fi
.SH "THREAD SAFETY"
.nf
Thread-safe if the pool was created with single_threaded = false.
Each sub-pool has its own lock. Frees of different size classes therefore
never contend with one another. Inside a tier that keeps a cache for each
thread, the common path takes no lock at all. An entry that one thread frees
can go to another thread.
.fi
.SH "SEE ALSO"
.BR ccol_mempool_free_entry (3),
.BR ccol_r_mempool_alloc_entry (3),
.BR ccol_r_mempool_create (3)
